---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import WaitlistForm from '~/components/widgets/WaitlistForm.astro';
import LivingProcess from '~/components/widgets/use-case/LivingProcess.astro';
import { seoFor } from '~/utils/seo';

const metadata = seoFor('/business-process-software');
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <article class="px-4 sm:px-6 mx-auto max-w-6xl pt-16 pb-4">
    <div
      class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert prose-headings:font-heading prose-headings:tracking-tight max-w-3xl mx-auto"
    >
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold tracking-wide text-primary">Business Process Software</p>
      <h1>Living Business Processes</h1>
      <p>
        Business process management software is bought to stop the process from living in someone's head. A year later
        the suite has a diagram, the application has the real steps, and the two disagree. The diagram was the project.
        The code is what the customer hits. Business process management tools that only draw the process leave you with
        a second copy to maintain.
      </p>
    </div>

    <LivingProcess class="mt-8" />

    <div
      class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert prose-headings:font-heading prose-headings:tracking-tight max-w-3xl mx-auto mt-10"
    >
      <h2>The Drift</h2>
      <p>
        Take fulfillment. The diagram says payment is checked before stock moves. The service reserves inventory on the
        way into the queue, because that was the bugfix that shipped on a Friday. Both are "the process." Neither knows
        about the other. The next audit, the next agent, and the next hire all pick one.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the process above, that rule is on the step it governs. Stock cannot be reserved until payment has cleared,
        and dispatch stays with the warehouse. The steps, the roles, and the work are the description the software is
        built from.
      </p>

      <h2>The Difference</h2>
      <p>
        A BPM suite runs the process on its own engine. DNA describes the process, and the systems you already run
        follow that description.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A BPM suite</strong> draws the process and runs it on its own engine. Work items, timers, and human tasks
          live there. That is the right tool when the suite is the system of record for the work: case management, long-running
          approvals, a queue that has no other home.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>DNA</strong> models the process as data. The docs, the application, the workflow, and the agent read that
          one model. DNA does not execute long-running workflows on an engine of its own. It tells the systems you already
          run who may do what, and it keeps the description those systems are built from.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you need a place where the work waits, buy a BPM engine. If the work already lives in your app and your
        agents, and the description of it keeps falling behind, the thing you are missing is the model.
      </p>

      <h2>Read more</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/blog/business-process-modeling">Business Process Modeling with DNA</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
  <div class="px-4 sm:px-6 mx-auto max-w-3xl pb-16">
    <WaitlistForm preselect="operations" />
  </div>
</Layout>
